# =============================================================================
# fac omnia … dum: a caught error ends the loop
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `fac omnia { … } cape err { … } dum cond` is an ordinary do-while `fac … dum`
#     loop today: the body runs, the handler wraps the whole loop, and a caught
#     `iace` leaves it (the `dum` test is not reached)
#   • the marker round-trips the formatter
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting the loop to continue after the handler — the handler wraps the
#     whole `fac … dum`, so a caught error ends it; to keep looping, catch
#     inside the body of a `dum` loop (see fac-omnia-dum-continue)
#
# See also: fac, cape, omnia, dum
# =============================================================================

# fac omnia … dum — the handler ends the loop
#
# GRAMMAR:
#   facStmt :← 'fac' 'omnia'? block cape_clause? ('dum' expr)?
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   iteratio 1 / iteratio 2 / iteratio 3 / interceptum / finis 3
#
# BACKEND:
#   Runner: plain fac … dum; the omnia marker is surface-only here.

incipit {
    varia numerus i ← 0
    fac omnia {
        i ← i + 1
        nota "iteratio §"(i)
        si i ≥ 3 {
            iace "tertia"
        }
    }
    cape err {
        nota "interceptum"
    } dum i ≺ 10
    nota "finis §"(i)
}
